  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species has a wide distribution as it is reported from New Caledonia and north-east Australia (Lizard Island), Papua New Guinea, and through Indonesia and north to the Philippines (Norman and Hochberg 2005, Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014). It has also been recorded from the Red Sea (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014). It has been recorded from relatively shallow waters of&#160;0.5 to at least 37 m (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014).",
    "population": "There is no information on the population of this species.",
    "habitats": "This day-active species is found on open plains of sand or mud, often adjacent to silt-laden river outflows (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014). The species often occupies the empty burrows of other animals, and may use these lairs temporarily or as a network of regular lairs within a home range. This species is best known for its mimicry of toxic species that co-occur in the same habitat: banded soles, sea snakes, and lionfish, for example, with many poses and behaviours not yet elucidated (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014).",
    "threats": "This species is traded for the aquarium trade and high fatality rates in the capture process would make this harvest a potential threat to this species (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014). However, at present, the scale of this is unknown. The species also occurs at relatively shallow depths and often close to river outflows which provides a potential source of pollution as a threat to this species.",
    "measures": "There are no conservation measures in place for this species. Additional research is required on this species to establish if harvesting for the aquarium trade may have an impact on the species, at least in parts of its range.",
    "use_trade": "This species is traded for the aquarium trade, and it is of increasingly high value to live trade due to the species increasing profile in documentaries, photography, etc.",
    "rationale": "<em>Thaumoctopus mimicus</em>&#160;has been assessed as Least Concern. This species has a wide distribution from Australia, New Guinea and Indonesia to the Gulf of Thailand and the South China Sea, and has also been recorded in the Red Sea. The species has been harvested for live specimens for the aquarium trade, this may have an impact on the species if take off levels are unsustainable in parts of its range, for example due to high fatality rates. Research is required to monitor this and establish whether the species can cope with the current level of harvesting.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "Type locality: New Caledonia, lagoon off north tip of island, 19°49’S, 163°48’E (Norman and Hochberg 2005)."
  },
